prompt Keaton gave no new instruction this lap; the baton and the inner prompt directed re-reading the lane's ground (ruled 20261002.142242) before assuming the queue was still empty.
think .loop-gates-only was absent at lap open, meaning the sentinel set at the end of the prior lap was cleared -- so the ruling's own instruction stood: re-read REDS %807 and %765 fresh, re-read fleet-claims.kyri off xy, and re-run the grep for a bare -v1/-v2 header constant in mantra/ and tally/ rather than trusting the prior lap's empty reading.
think the narrow grep (appendSlice-style header dispatch) found nothing new, matching the last three laps -- but widening the pattern to any v1/v2-suffixed string literal surfaced six hits, four of them elder (receipt_offer.rye schema_v1, predating %765's ruling by three days; mantra-weave and mantra-commit read-paths, already molted) and two freshly dated inside consent_replay.rye's grant_schema and revoke_schema, authored 20261001.201747, five days after %765 was ruled.
think pressed on the boundary rather than assuming it: grepped mantra/ and tally/ for both schema strings and found only the module's own witness, which names the constants rather than the literal, so no dispatch line or external store depends on the elder spelling -- the one difference that makes this molt cheaper than mantra-weave's or mantra-commit's, which both kept a live elder-read path.
obs chose to molt grant_schema and revoke_schema to the one-clock stamp read at the moment of the edit (20261002.180446), the same convention the commit-header molt used, rather than the constants' own authorship stamp -- the molt date names when the tree corrected itself, not when the mistake was made.
obs left receipt_offer.rye's schema_v1 untouched: it predates the %765 ruling and is therefore an elder header under accrete-never-break, not a fresh violation of a rule that did not yet exist when it was written.
rota lap 7591, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. The reading: distinguishing an elder header (keeps its name forever) from a fresh one born after the ruling (molts now, cheaply) is exactly a boundary question -- the hand presses on "when was this written" and the edge either holds or gives. It held cleanly here: one file five days late, one file three days early, and the line between them decided the whole lap.
